{{
A rotate and insert: the last child moves to the front and a new child is inserted right after it, as a table does for "rotate and insert". The new child must end up after the moved one, not where the moved one used to be.

Each child is its own computation with its own tag, so the run correlates them by identity, as a keyed list does.
}}
tests.relocateAndInsert : [Result]
tests.relocateAndInsert =
  ReconcileTest.run do
    use Text ++
    child tag = compute (Some tag) do tag
    runReconcile [] do
      a = child "A"
      b = child "B"
      c = child "C"
      a ++ b ++ c
    expectEqual lastTree (T "ABC" [T "A" [], T "B" [], T "C" []])
    runReconcile [] do
      c = child "C"
      n = child "N"
      a = child "A"
      b = child "B"
      c ++ n ++ a ++ b
    expectEqual lastTree (T "CNAB" [T "C" [], T "N" [], T "A" [], T "B" []])

{{ A swap of the first and last of five children, with a new child after the new first. }}
tests.swapAndInsert : [Result]
tests.swapAndInsert =
  ReconcileTest.run do
    child tag = compute (Some tag) do tag
    runReconcile [] do
      List.map child ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"] |> Text.join ""
    runReconcile [] do
      List.map child ["E", "N", "B", "C", "D", "A"] |> Text.join ""
    expectEqual lastTree (T "ENBCDA" [T "E" [], T "N" [], T "B" [], T "C" [], T "D" [], T "A" []])

ReleaseNotes : Doc
ReleaseNotes =
  {{
  * Rename internal {type Cell} functions to be unique in more contexts.
  * Keyed children are reconciled in `O(n log n)`. Siblings are compared by their last key segment, the common prefix stays put, and the children whose old positions form a longest increasing run ({Incremental.reconcile.stableIndices}) stay still while the others are relocated after their new predecessors.
  * Fixed: a new child now moves with its predecessor. A rotate and insert, or a swap with an insert after it, used to leave the new child where its predecessor had been, because it was placed before the predecessor was relocated. `tests.relocateAndInsert` and `tests.swapAndInsert` cover it.
  }}
